{"arguments": [
 {"id": "G1", "name": "John Cook's thesis: Paul taught bodily resurrection", "conclusion": "Paul's view of the resurrection is that he saw the same bodily appearances of Jesus described in the Gospels (Matthew, Luke, John), not a ghostly or non-bodily view.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas (reporting John Cook)", "premises": ["Cook states his thesis up front in his resurrection volume, published in the respected German Wissenschaftliche series.", "Cook explicitly denies that Paul teaches a 'Casper' (ghostly) view of resurrection appearances.", "Cook holds that Paul teaches the same bodily appearances found in Matthew, Luke, and John."], "strength": {"st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "my thesis is that Paul's view of the resurrection is that he saw the same body as described in the Gospels"}, "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "He says, \"Before you read, I'm going to give you my thesis statement up front.\""}, "st3": {"v": 1, "quote": "they're both included in the uh German uh Wissenschaftliche series, which is now hundreds of volumes"}, "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "He teaches the same bodily appearances Matthew, Luke, and John do."}, "st5": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "He means not this wispy Mike Mike wrote a some treatments of this and he says um Mike called those views like I call them Jedi Jesus."}, "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "He starts at the beginning."}}, "prior_art": "John Cook, 'Empty Tomb' volumes on crucifixion and resurrection (Wissenschaftliche series)", "originality": {"or1": {"v": 2, "quote": "He's doing mostly Greco- Greco-Roman historiography.", "why": "Cook's historiographical method applied to Paul's resurrection view is not a restatement of prior apologetic arguments."}, "or2": {"v": 2, "quote": "they're both included in the uh German uh Wissenschaftliche series, which is now hundreds of volumes", "why": "Cites publication venue and Cook's Greco-Roman historiographical method as support."}, "or3": {"v": 1, "quote": "He's doing mostly Greco- Greco-Roman historiography.", "why": "Connects Greco-Roman historiography to NT resurrection studies."}, "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization or model added."}}, "weakest_link": "The premise that Cook's thesis accurately represents Paul's view rests only on Cook's assertion, not on exegesis of the texts.", "develop_next": "Provide the actual Pauline texts Cook cites (e.g., 1 Cor 15) to show the bodily reading is exegetically forced.", "case_map": ["K16", "F4"], "domain": "history"},
 {"id": "G2", "name": "Bodily resurrection view is now widely accepted", "conclusion": "The view that Paul believed in bodily resurrection is now very widely taught, despite being extremely unpopular when Cook's books first appeared.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas", "premises": ["Cook's bodily-resurrection thesis was extremely unpopular when his volumes first came out.", "The fact that Paul believes in bodily resurrection is now very widely taught by scholars today."], "strength": {"st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "now it's kind of taken the day, the fact that Paul believes in bodily resurrection is very widely taught today"}, "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "That's extremely impo- unpopular when this these books first came out or even after."}, "st3": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "Very widely taught by the guys."}, "st5": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "now it's kind of taken the day, the fact that Paul believes in bodily resurrection is very widely taught today"}, "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "Very widely taught by the guys."}}, "prior_art": "N.T. Wright, 'The Resurrection of the Son of God', 2003", "originality": {"or1": {"v": 1, "quote": "now it's kind of taken the day, the fact that Paul believes in bodily resurrection is very widely taught today", "why": "A variant of the standard scholarly-consensus claim, updated to the post-Cook era."}, "or2": {"v": 1, "quote": "That's extremely impo- unpopular when this these books first came out or even after.", "why": "Adds the historical reception data about Cook's unpopularity."}, "or3": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No cross-domain connection."}, "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization."}}, "weakest_link": "The consensus claim is asserted without naming or counting the scholars who hold it.", "develop_next": "Give a concrete list or survey of scholars now teaching the bodily view to substantiate 'very widely taught'.", "case_map": ["K16", "F4"], "domain": "history"},
 {"id": "G3", "name": "Dale Allison concedes Paul meant bodily resurrection", "conclusion": "Even a scholar not personally committed to the bodily view, Dale Allison, affirms that Paul meant bodily resurrection.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas (reporting Dale Allison)", "premises": ["Dale Allison says Paul believed in bodily resurrection appearances like the Gospels do.", "Allison states he is more open to non-bodily things and is not on the bodily side himself.", "Allison insists one must distinguish what Paul said from what Bultmann said about Paul."], "strength": {"st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "Dale Allison said Paul believed in bodily resurrection appearance like the Gospels do."}, "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "Dale says, \"I'm more open to non-bodily things but I am telling you even though I'm not on that interpret- I'm not in the bodily side in my view, I think Paul meant bodily resurrection."}, "st3": {"v": 1, "quote": "You have got to make this distinguish between what Paul said and somebody else going \"Well, Bultmann says Paul believed bodily resurrection.\""}, "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "I think Paul meant bodily resurrection."}, "st5": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st6": {"v": 2, "quote": "I'm more open to non-bodily things but I am telling you even though I'm not on that interpret- I'm not in the bodily side in my view, I think Paul meant bodily resurrection."}, "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "Dale Allison said Paul believed in bodily resurrection appearance like the Gospels do."}}, "prior_art": "Dale Allison, 'Resurrecting Jesus', 2005", "originality": {"or1": {"v": 1, "quote": "Dale Allison said Paul believed in bodily resurrection appearance like the Gospels do.", "why": "A variant of the standard critical-concession argument, citing a specific non-committed scholar."}, "or2": {"v": 2, "quote": "I'm more open to non-bodily things but I am telling you even though I'm not on that interpret- I'm not in the bodily side in my view, I think Paul meant bodily resurrection.", "why": "Uses Allison's self-described non-commitment as evidence, which prior art does not foreground."}, "or3": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No cross-domain connection."}, "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization."}}, "weakest_link": "The claim that Allison is 'not on the bodily side' is asserted by Habermas, not demonstrated from Allison's own texts.", "develop_next": "Quote Allison's own methodological caveats verbatim to show he is a genuinely hostile witness.", "case_map": ["K16", "F4"], "domain": "history"},
 {"id": "G4", "name": "Bultmann affirmed Paul taught bodily resurrection", "conclusion": "Bultmann himself acknowledged that Paul believed in bodily resurrection, even though Bultmann did not accept it.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas", "premises": ["Bultmann can say Paul believed in bodily resurrection while personally rejecting it.", "This is the distinction Allison was drawing between Paul's view and Bultmann's own view."], "strength": {"st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "But Bultmann can say Paul believed it, but I don't."}, "st2": {"v": 1, "quote": "You have got to make this distinguish between what Paul said and somebody else going \"Well, Bultmann says Paul believed bodily resurrection.\""}, "st3": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "But Bultmann can say Paul believed it, but I don't."}, "st5": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "But Bultmann can say Paul believed it, but I don't."}, "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st8": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}}, "prior_art": "Rudolf Bultmann, 'Theology of the New Testament', 1948-1953", "originality": {"or1": {"v": 1, "quote": "But Bultmann can say Paul believed it, but I don't.", "why": "A variant of the standard 'even Bultmann conceded' move."}, "or2": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No new evidence added."}, "or3": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No cross-domain connection."}, "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization."}}, "weakest_link": "No citation to any Bultmann text is given; the concession is asserted from memory.", "develop_next": "Cite the exact Bultmann passage where he grants Paul's bodily view.", "case_map": ["K16", "F4"], "domain": "history"},
 {"id": "G5", "name": "Cook's narrow view is credible, not fundamentalist", "conclusion": "John Cook's adoption of a narrow bodily-resurrection view is a stunning and credible scholarly move, not the product of fundamentalist bias.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas", "premises": ["Cook states up front that he is taking a narrow view.", "Cook would not be blamed as one of the 'fighting fundy' type guys.", "German scholarship still carries a certain credibility of scholarship."], "strength": {"st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "that was a stunning one"}, "st2": {"v": 2, "quote": "you wouldn't blame him with being one of these fighting fundy type guys at all"}, "st3": {"v": 1, "quote": "German scholarship still means German scholarship."}, "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "you wouldn't blame him with being one of these fighting fundy type guys at all"}, "st5": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "you wouldn't blame him with being one of these fighting fundy type guys at all"}, "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st8": {"v": 1, "quote": "German scholarship still means German scholarship."}}, "prior_art": "John Cook, Wissenschaftliche-series volumes on crucifixion and resurrection", "originality": {"or1": {"v": 1, "quote": "you wouldn't blame him with being one of these fighting fundy type guys at all", "why": "A variant of the 'hostile witness' credibility move, applied to Cook specifically."}, "or2": {"v": 1, "quote": "German scholarship still means German scholarship.", "why": "Adds the venue-credibility consideration."}, "or3": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No cross-domain connection."}, "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization."}}, "weakest_link": "The inference from 'not a fundy' to 'credible scholarship' is a reputation appeal, not evidence.", "develop_next": "Show Cook's method is critical by citing his non-confessional handling of Greco-Roman sources.", "case_map": ["K16", "F4"], "domain": "history"},
 {"id": "G6", "name": "Wright compares resurrection knowability to Caesar's Rubicon", "conclusion": "The resurrection is as historically knowable as Julius Caesar crossing the Rubicon.", "speaker": "Gary Habermas (reporting Tom Wright)", "premises": ["Tom Wright made a comment comparing the knowability of the resurrection to Caesar's crossing of the Rubicon.", "Habermas and his interlocutor recognize the quote and attribute it to Wright."], "strength": {"st1": {"v": 2, "quote": "the resurrection is as knowable as the Rubicon"}, "st2": {"v": 1, "quote": "There's it's as solid to me as the Jesus raised from the dead as a Julius Caesar crossed the uh Is that Pardon me?"}, "st3": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st4": {"v": 1, "quote": "the resurrection is as knowable as the Rubicon"}, "st5": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st6": {"v": 1, "quote": "Now, because I don't buy it, it didn't stun me as being super positive"}, "st7": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}, "st8": {"v": 0, "quote": ""}}, "prior_art": "N.T. Wright, 'The Resurrection of the Son of God', 2003", "originality": {"or1": {"v": 1, "quote": "the resurrection is as knowable as the Rubicon", "why": "A variant of Wright's historical-knowability claim, compressed into an analogy."}, "or2": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No new evidence added."}, "or3": {"v": 1, "quote": "the resurrection is as knowable as the Rubicon", "why": "Connects Roman political history to resurrection historiography."}, "or4": {"v": 0, "quote": "", "why": "No formalization."}}, "weakest_link": "The analogy is reported second-hand and never unpacked; no criterion of knowability is given.", "develop_next": "State the historical criteria under which the Rubicon and the resurrection are equally knowable.", "case_map": ["K16", "F4"], "domain": "history"}
]}
